#{ ===========================================================================
   TAKING, GIVING, KNOWING -- the custody relations at a work's boundary.

   One verb, one relation, everywhere (0.2):

     knows:  ACQUAINTANCE. The work may read what it is told; it takes no
             responsibility and confers none. Knowing creates no debt --
             the known must simply outlive the knower's use.
     takes:  CUSTODY IN.  The work RECEIVES the object; the giver's name
             dies; the taker now HAS -- and owes the end of what it has
             (give onward or destruct, 0.3).
     gives:  CUSTODY OUT. The lawful end of having: at give: the
             gives-bundle moves WHOLE to the caller, who has it now.

   Giving is moving, never copying: one object, one debt, one haver at a
   time. The trace of responsibility is the chain of these three verbs.

   HONESTY NOTE: the call-site 'give' marker and the ledger that PROVES
   the chain bind the future type document (SEMANTICS 23, deferred); the
   for:-from: loop already spells its two custody flavours (13.5).
   =========================================================================== }

score : class has: { points: float; } {
    from : work
}

+score.from : work(knows: start          #{ told, not owned: acquaintance }
                   gives: s : score      #{ custody OUT: the caller will HAVE }
                   signals: negative) {
    if: start < 0 { exit: negative; }
    s = start;
    give:                              # the give happens HERE, implicitly
}

#{ A disposal work is the mirror: custody IN, and the debt ends inside. }

-score : work(takes: s : score) {        #{ custody IN: the debt arrives }
    give:
}

#{ knows: never carries debt -- 'bonus' below reads its inputs and gives a
   fresh value; nothing is taken, nothing is owed, nothing to discharge. }

bonus : work(knows: base, factor
             gives: total) {
    total = base * factor;
    give:
}

Demo : behavior has: { s: float; t: float; } {
    SHOW => {
        b.s = score.from(start = 40) else: { negative => ; }
        b.t = bonus(b.s, 2);
    }
}
